this is the Boeing factory near Seattle  where they make the 737 max planes  journalists invited inside for the first  time since they were grounded following  two deadly crashes as Boeing announces  updates to make the aircraft safer we're  going to do everything that we can do to  ensure that accidents like these never  happen again we're working with  customers and regulators to restore  faith in our industry and also to  reaffirm our commitment to safety and to  earning the trust of the flying public  the two incidents more recently with  Ethiopian Airlines and before that with  Lyon air in Indonesia have been  described by experts as similar both  planes plunging towards the earth  shortly after takeoff investigators said  the lion air pilots battled in vain to  override something called the M caste  system which was fed faulty data and  repeatedly pushed the nose of the  aircraft down Boeing says it's new fixes  make MKS less powerful and less prone to  error  they've also proposed extra training for  pilots many of whom are still furious  that Boeing didn't tell them about M  Cass before they launched the max planes  we believe that this was a serious  breach of trust between the pilot group  and the manufacturer we do feel that we  absolutely should have been made aware  of the existence of the system  absolutely should have been aware of the  way it could potentially malfunction it  was inexcusable and it absolutely should  not have happened the world's largest  commercial plane manufacturer has a lot  of work to do  Boeing may still be building its Mac's  planes but it cannot say when those  planes are going to be back in service  that is up to regulators across the  world who need to recertify the plane as  safe that might be complicated with US  politicians accusing the Federal  Aviation Authority of giving Boeing too  much freedom to approve its own designs  the fact is that the FAA decided to do  safety on the cheap which is neither  nor safe and put the Fox in charge the  henhouse with reputations faith in  flying and a lot of cash on the line  these questions will keep on coming  Hannah Thomas Peter Sky News in Renton  Washington State
 

